    <h2 id="case-poc-title">06 Compositional generalization</h2>
    <section
      id="case-poc-setting"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="case-poc-setting-title"
    >
      <h3 id="case-poc-setting-title">Evaluation setting</h3>
      <div class="report-prose">
        <p>
          Beyond Ebench, we further evaluate a compositional unseen task that requires placing a bookmark and a pen on the
          same open book sequentially. Bookmark on book and pen-placement tasks with other
          targets (in a pen holder or on a ruler) appear in the robot-policy training set, whereas
          pen on book does not. This setting therefore tests more than the
          sequencing of two familiar tasks: the combined objective includes an sub-task absent
          from the robot-policy training data. Each method is evaluated over 10 rollouts and GPT-6-Astra receives
          no in-context demonstrations. The prescribed sequence places the bookmark first and then the pen and
          the partial credit of 0.5 points is awarded for correctly completing the first object placement in the prescribed sequence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section
      id="case-poc-analysis"
      class="case-study-section"
      aria-labelledby="case-poc-analysis-title"
    >
      <h3 id="case-poc-analysis-title">
        Atomic task generalization does not guarantee compositional generalization
      </h3>
      <div class="case-study-content"></div>
    </section>
